  "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/stack-data-structure": "A Stack is a linear data structure that follows a particular order in which the operations are performed. The order is LIFO(Last In First Out) which implies that the element that is inserted last, comes out first.\n\nIt behaves like a stack of plates, where the last plate added is the first one to be removed. Pushing an element onto the stack is like adding a new plate on top. Popping an element removes the top plate from the stack.\n\n**Basics **\n\n**Basics**\n\n**Implementations in Different Languages**\n\n**Implementations in Different Languages**\n\n**Easy Problems **\n\n**Easy Problems**\n\n- The Celebrity Problem\n- Queue using Stacks\n- Two stacks in an array\n- Infix to Postfix\n- Prefix to Infix\n- Prefix to Postfix\n- Postfix to Prefix\n- Postfix to Infix\n- Infix To Prefix\n- Check for balanced parentheses\n- Evaluation of Postfix Expression\n- Reverse a stack using recursion\n- Reversing the first K of a Queue\n- A DS with O(1) Operations\n\n**Medium Problems **\n\n**Medium Problems**\n\n- k Stacks in an Array\n- Mergable Stack\n- Stack using Queues\n- Previous Smaller Element\n- Next Greater Element\n- Stock Span Problem\n- Buildings Facing Sun\n- Next Smaller of next Greater\n- Next Greater Frequency\n- Max product of indexes of greater on left and right\n- Iterative Tower of Hanoi\n- Sort a Stack\n- Reverse a Stack\n- Delete middle of a stack\n- Check for queue sortable\n- Check for stack sortable\n- Index of closing bracket for a given opening bracket\n- Max Diff between nearest left and right smaller\n- Delete consecutive same words\n\n**Hard Problems**\n\n**Hard Problems**\n\n- Largest Rectangle in a Histogram\n- Sum of Max of all Subarrays\n- Max of Mins of every window size\n- Stack that supports getMin()\n- Stack with max frequency\n- Longest valid substring\n- Check Redundant Bracket\n- Stack Permutations\n- Remove brackets containing + and – operators\n\n**Quick Links :**",
  "https://www.acceldata.io/blog/stack-data-structure-explained-how-lifo-drives-modern-computing": "As of June 2022, more than 500 hours of video were uploaded on YouTube every minute, and the global digital universe is expected to expand significantly by 2025, driven by algorithms that influence decisions in every industry.\n\nConsider undoing a single step in your document editor or retrieving the most recent transaction on a banking app. These actions might seem trivial but rely on the Last In, First Out (LIFO) principle—a hallmark of stacks. From simplifying software design to optimizing real-world systems, stacks are indispensable.\n\nThis article explores stacks, their operations, and real-world implementations, highlighting how this fundamental structure powers modern computing efficiency.\n\n**What Is Stack Data Structure?**\n\nStack is a simple data structure that follows the Last In, First Out (LIFO) principle. It is akin to a stack of browser tabs: new ones open on top, and when closing, it is always the most recent one.\n\nStacks operate through a single point using a push/pop mechanism. The \"top\" is where all adding (push) and removing (pop) happens. Thus, stacks are efficient and easy to manage.\n\nYou see stacks in action in everyday computing tasks:\n\n- **Undo in apps:**Editing text or images? Each change is added to a stack. Undo removes the last change first.\n- **Browser history:**Every page you visit is pushed onto a stack. Clicking back pops the most recent page.\n- **Function calls in programming:**Recursive functions push each call onto a stack, returning step-by-step when done.\n- **Syntax validation:**Checking if brackets or parentheses match in code relies on a stack to track openings and closings.\n- **Backtracking in games:**Games use stacks to track previous moves when exploring mazes or puzzles.\n\nThese examples indicate how stacks simplify tasks behind the scenes. Next, we will explore how they work and the operations that power them.\n\n**Core Operations of Stack**\n\nPush, pop, and peek are at the heart of a stack data structure. These simple yet powerful actions form the foundation for several real-world software functionalities, from undo buttons to expression evaluation.\n\nEfficient stack operations are critical for maintaining the health of data pipelines, ensuring seamless flow and transformation of data.\n\nLet us break them down with examples.\n\n**1. Push operation**\n\nThe push operation adds an element to the top of the stack.\n\nExample:** **Think of typing text into a word processor. Each letter you type is “pushed” onto an undo stack. If you need to undo, the last letter added will be the first one removed. \n\n**Algorithm**\n\n- Check if the stack is full (for array-based implementations).\n- If not, increment the top index.\n- Add the new element to the top.\n\n**2. Pop operation**\n\nThe pop operation removes and returns the top element from the stack.\n\nExample:** **When navigating through browser tabs, the most recently opened tab is “popped” off when you close it. The stack ensures you are always working with the most recent item first.\n\n**Algorithm**\n\n- Check if the stack is empty.\n- If not, retrieve the top element.\n- Decrement the top index.\n\n**3. Peek operation**\n\nThe peek operation retrieves the topmost element without removing it.\n\nExample:** **In the calculator app, the stack holds operators during expression evaluation. The app peeks at the top of the stack to decide the next step based on operator precedence, ensuring the calculations are correct.\n\n**4. Utility functions**\n\n- **isEmpty:**Checks if the stack is empty.- **Scenario:**Before popping or peeking, a program ensures there is something to work with.- **\n\n- **isFull:**Checks if the stack has reached its maximum size for array-based stacks.- **Scenario:**Imagine saving drafts of an email in a fixed storage space. You need to check if there is room for more before saving a new version.\n\nThese operations and functions together make stacks flexible and efficient for solving numerous computational problems. Next, let us explore how stacks are implemented using arrays and linked lists.\n\n\n**Implementing Stacks: Array vs. Linked List**\n\nArrays and linked lists are the two common approaches while implementing a stack. Each has its strengths and limitations, making them suitable for different scenarios.\n\nSelecting the right implementation for stacks can directly impact the performance of large-scale systems, where data reliability and scalability are paramount.\n\n**1. Array-based implementation**\n\nArray implementation uses contiguous memory locations, where each element is stored side-by-side.\n\n\nImagine you are organizing books on a small shelf with a fixed number of slots. Each slot represents an index in an array. You can quickly add or remove books from the end, but if the shelf is full, there is no room for more unless you replace the entire shelf.\n\n**Advantages**\n\n- **Fast indexing:**Direct access to elements using their index.\n- **Predictable layout:**Memory allocation is straightforward and consistent.\n\n**Limitations**\n\n- **Fixed size:**You risk overflow if the stack exceeds the pre-defined size.\n- **Inflexibility:**Resizing the stack requires creating a new array, which can be computationally expensive.\n\n**Real-world application**Array-based stacks are ideal in environments where memory usage is predictable, such as embedded systems or real-time applications like sensors in IoT devices. \n\n\n**2. Linked list-based implementation**\n\nIn a linked list implementation, we represent the stack as a series of nodes. Each node contains the data and a pointer to the next node.\n\n\nImagine you are stacking plates at a buffet, but there is no fixed shelf. Instead, each plate rests on another. You can dynamically add or remove plates without worrying about a fixed limit.\n\n**Advantages**\n\n- **Dynamic resizing:**There is no predetermined size; the stack grows and shrinks as needed.\n- **Efficient memory usage:**Memory is allocated only when needed, avoiding wastage of space.\n\n**Limitations**\n\n- **Pointer overhead:**Each node requires extra memory for the pointer, making it less space-efficient.\n- **Slightly slower operations:**Manipulating pointers adds a small performance cost compared to arrays.\n\n**Real-world application**Linked list stacks are well-suited for managing recursive function calls where the depth of recursion is unpredictable, such as complex mathematical computations or solving mazes.\n\n**Applications of Stack Data Structure**\n\nStacks might seem simple, but they are vital to many software systems, simplifying tasks such as parsing expressions, managing function calls, and validating syntax.\n\nAdvanced observability platforms can monitor stack-based systems within data engineering workflows, ensuring every process runs efficiently and without disruption.\n\nHere’s how companies rely on stacks:\n\n**Expression parsing**\n\nStacks convert infix expressions (e.g., A + B * C*) to postfix (ABC*+) for efficient evaluation.**Example**: Microsoft Word utilizes a stack-based mechanism to manage the sequence of user actions, enabling efficient reversal of operations. Each action is pushed onto a stack. Invoking 'undo' pops the most recent action, reverting the document to its previous state. This stack-based approach ensures that users can seamlessly backtrack through their recent edits, enhancing the document editing experience.\n\n**Function call management**\n\nCall stacks track active functions and return addresses, which are crucial for recursion.**Example**: Airbnb’s pricing algorithms use recursive calls to analyze historical data and preferences. Call stacks manage these calculations for accuracy across millions of properties.\n\n**Syntax validation**\n\nStacks ensure code integrity by matching parentheses, brackets, and braces.**Example**: GitHub’s editor uses stacks to validate syntax in real time, catching errors as developers code. These examples indicate how stacks power tools we use daily, from spreadsheets to complex platforms.\n\n**Challenges and Limitations**\n\nStacks are versatile; however, their implementation entails unique challenges. Understanding these limitations and their solutions can help optimize stack performance in different scenarios.\n\nLet us consider some common challenges:\n\n\nBy addressing these challenges strategically, you can ensure stacks function efficiently, even in resource-constrained environments.\n\n**Optimizing Stack Performance with Acceldata**\n\nStacks are foundational to countless computing processes, from managing function calls to validating syntax and enabling efficient expression parsing. Despite their simplicity, stacks are pivotal to building robust and scalable systems.\n\nEfficient monitoring and optimization are crucial to harness the full potential of stacks in complex environments. Acceldata's data observability platform offers deep insights into stack-based operations, ensuring your data pipelines and system processes are both efficient and reliable.\n\nWith advanced support for real-time data processing, Acceldata ensures your stack-based systems can handle the demands of modern computing with ease. With Acceldata, you gain the ability to identify bottlenecks, enhance scalability, and maintain data integrity effortlessly.\n\nReady to transform your stack-based systems? Book a demo with Acceldata today and discover how its platform can elevate your data infrastructure.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)\n\n**1. What is a stack in data structure with a real-life example?**\n\nA stack is a data structure that works on the Last In, First Out (LIFO) principle—like a stack of plates. The last plate placed on top is the first one you remove. In computing, this is used in things like undo actions in word processors or tracking function calls in code.\n\n\n**2. Why are stacks important in real-world applications?**\n\nStacks help manage tasks in a predictable and reversible order. For example, browser history uses a stack so users can go back to the last page. They're also essential in programming for handling function calls, syntax checking, and managing user actions.\n\n\n3. What are the key stack operations?\n\n3. What are the key stack operations?\n\nThe main operations are:\n\n- **Push**(add an item),\n- **Pop**(remove the top item),\n- **Peek**(view the top item without removing it),\n\nThese simple actions allow complex systems like compilers or calculators to work correctly.\n\n\n**4. When should I use a stack instead of a queue?**\n\nUse a stack when you need to reverse actions or manage tasks in a \"last in, first out\" order—like backtracking in games or undo features. Queues are better for tasks that follow a \"first in, first out\" sequence, like processing jobs in order.\n\n\n**5. What’s the difference between array and linked list stack implementations?**\n\n- **Array-based stacks**have a fixed size and are fast but can overflow.\n- **Linked list stacks**grow as needed and are flexible but use more memory.\n\nChoose based on your application's needs—fixed capacity or dynamic growth.\n\n\n**6. How do stacks help in syntax validation?**\n\nStacks help by keeping track of opening and closing symbols (like brackets or parentheses). As you type code, the stack checks if each opening symbol has a corresponding closing one in the correct order—making it vital for code editors.\n\n\n**7. What are common stack-related issues in coding?**\n\nSome challenges include stack overflows in recursion, memory limits in array-based stacks, and performance issues at scale. Using best practices and monitoring tools can help identify and resolve these problems early.\n\n\n**8. How does Acceldata help optimize stack-based systems?**\n\nAcceldata provides real-time monitoring and insights into stack operations within your data pipelines. It helps detect performance bottlenecks, prevent errors, and ensure that your stack-based applications run smoothly at scale.\n\n\n**9. What is Agentic Data Management and how is it different?**\n\nAgentic Data Management, as offered by Acceldata, uses AI agents that don’t just observe but also act. These agents monitor stacks and data flows, detect anomalies, and take action to fix issues automatically—without waiting on humans.\n\n\n**10. Why do modern data platforms need stack observability?**\n\nStacks play a critical role in how data moves and is processed. Without observability, it's hard to detect delays, errors, or inefficiencies. Platforms like Acceldata use intelligent agents to ensure every stack-based task runs with accuracy, speed, and transparency.",
  "https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/intro-to-stacks-data-structure-and-algorithm-tutorial": "Understanding the stack data structure is important for anyone interested in computer science. It is a key data structure used in many programs and also coding challenges. Understanding stacks can help you in technical interviews.\n\nWe just posted a course on the freeCodeCamp.org YouTube channel that will help you master the stack data structure. The course is a deep dive into one of the most fundamental and powerful data structures in computer science. Parth from Destination FAANG developed this course.\n\nStacks are dynamic data structures that follow the Last In, First Out (LIFO) principle, where the last element added to the stack is the first one to be removed. This course, enriched with diagrams and code examples, will provide you with a solid understanding of stacks and prepare you to ace those crucial interview questions.\n\nHere are the sections in the coruse:\n\n**Introduction to Stacks**\n\nThe course begins with an introduction to the stack data structure, explaining its LIFO nature and its analogy to a stack of plates. You'll learn about the basic operations of push (adding an item to the top), pop (removing the top item), and peek (viewing the top item without removing it), illustrated with Java examples:\n\n```\nStack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();\nstack.push(10); // Pushes 10 on the stack\nstack.push(20); // Pushes 20 on the stack\nstack.pop();    // Removes the top item (20)\nint top = stack.peek(); // Returns 10 without removing it\n```\n### Implementation, Operations, and Use Cases\n\nThis section covers the internal workings of stacks, showcasing how to implement a stack in Java using arrays or linked lists. You'll explore advanced stack operations and delve into practical use cases, such as undo mechanisms in text editors, parsing expressions, and backtracking algorithms.\n\n### When to Use Stacks\n\nUnderstanding when and where to apply stacks is crucial. This part of the course will guide you through scenarios where stacks shine, including function call management, syntax parsing, and maintaining histories in applications.\n\n### Ace Your Interviews with These Stack Questions\n\nThe course includes a dedicated section to prepare you for interviews, focusing on the 10 most popular stack-related questions. Each problem is broken down, explained, and solved with Java code to enhance your problem-solving skills and stack understanding.\n\n- **Daily Temperatures**: Learn how to use a stack to find the number of days until a warmer temperature.\n- **Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation**: Decode and evaluate expressions in Reverse Polish Notation using stacks.\n- **Valid Parentheses**: Use a stack to check for the validity of parentheses in a string.\n- **Min Stack**: Design a stack that supports push, pop, top, and retrieving the minimum element in constant time.\n- **Generate Parentheses**: Discover how to generate all combinations of well-formed parentheses with stacks.\n- **Car Fleet**: Understand how to apply stacks to solve complex problems like the car fleet challenge.\n- **Minimum Remove to Make Valid Parentheses**: Use stacks to balance parentheses in a string efficiently.\n- **Largest Rectangle in Histogram**: Tackle this classic problem with a stack-based approach to find the largest rectangular area.\n- **Longest Valid Parentheses**: Learn to use stacks to find the length of the longest valid parentheses substring.\n- **Max Stack**: Design a max stack that supports all regular stack operations along with retrieving the stack's maximum element.\n\n### Your Path to Stack Mastery\n\nBy the end of this course, you'll have a deep understanding of stacks, equipped with the knowledge to implement and utilize them effectively in various scenarios. Whether you're preparing for an interview or looking to solidify your understanding of data structures, this course offers a comprehensive guide to mastering stacks with real-world applications and interview-ready questions.\n\nWatch the full course on the freeCodeCamp.org YouTube channel (3-hour watch).",
